text:
marta: Heads up — I have a small repro for a flaky token refresh + retry loop in the Kotlin sample app on Android.

marta: Repo: github.com/redwood/sample-android (branch: auth-repro). Environment: Android 11 emulator, Kotlin SDK 0.9.2, Retrofit 2.9.0, OkHttp 4.9.3.

alex: Can you paste the minimal logs or the failing code path?

marta: Logs show many parallel 401s and then multiple refresh attempts:
I/OkHttp: <-- 401 Unauthorized http://api.local/v1/foo
I/OkHttp: refreshToken() started
I/OkHttp: refreshToken() completed -> new token
I/OkHttp: <-- 401 Unauthorized http://api.local/v1/bar
... then we see duplicated refresh calls and some requests end up retrying forever :facepalm: 

josh: This is a common pattern when you do token refresh inside an Interceptor. Interceptors run per-request and if 10 requests get 401 at once they all try to refresh. Use an OkHttp Authenticator for 401 handling or a singleflight/Mutex around refresh.

marta: Example of the problematic interceptor I had:
```kotlin
class AuthInterceptor(private val store: TokenStore) : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val req = chain.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer ${store.accessToken}")
      .build()

    val resp = chain.proceed(req)
    if (resp.code == 401) {
      // naive refresh
      store.refreshToken()
      val retried = req.newBuilder()
        .header("Authorization", "Bearer ${store.accessToken}")
        .build()
      return chain.proceed(retried)
    }
    return resp
  }
}
```

marta: That exact approach races and can trigger refresh spamming. Authenticator-based approach is better because it's invoked only on authentication challenges and you return a new request. Example:
```kotlin
class TokenAuthenticator(private val store: TokenStore) : Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    synchronized(store) { // simple guard
      if (store.isExpired()) {
        val ok = store.refreshTokenBlocking()
        if (!ok) return null
      }
      return response.request.newBuilder()
        .header("Authorization", "Bearer ${store.accessToken}")
        .build()
    }
  }
}
```

emily: Also watch out for timeouts and OkHttp retries. Default `retryOnConnectionFailure(true)` can re-execute POSTs; for non-idempotent endpoints set `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` and tune `callTimeout`, `connectTimeout`, `readTimeout`.

marta: Android quirk: when app goes background/network toggles we saw stale pooled connections causing `Canceled` exceptions that interact poorly with our retry logic. Adding `connectionPool.evictAll()` during network change helped in the sample.

alex: Sounds actionable. Can you add this to the Kotlin sample app + a small integration test that simulates parallel 401s?

marta: Will open a PR with Authenticator + sample test + README notes on timeouts and retryOnConnectionFailure. Also include an opt-in helper `SdkAuth.singleflight()` to avoid extra complexity.

